Import Word, LibreOffice, Markdown or plain-text files into your project. For a manuscript
returned by a reader, see [bringing back edits and comments](:help-round-trip). For Manuskript
or Plume Creator, see [importing projects](:help-import-projects).

## Import your files

1. Choose *Work ▸ Import from ▸ Documents (Markdown, Word, ODT)…*.
   You can also right-click in the Binder and choose *Import here…*.
2. On *Files*, drop your files or click *Browse…*. Supported files are `.docx`, `.odt`, `.md`
   and `.txt`. Put them in the order you want, then click *Next*.
3. On *Review*, check the proposed books, chapters and scenes. Change an item in the *Type*
   column if needed. Uncheck *Import* to leave out an item and everything inside it.
4. On *Destination*, choose where to put the imported writing. It goes inside a folder you
   select, or just after any other selected item.
5. Click *Import*. Nothing is added to the project before this step. Afterwards, the message
   offers *Undo* for a few seconds.

If the files contain writing already in your project, a *Merge* step may appear. See
[bringing back edits and comments](:help-round-trip) before continuing.

## Check how headings become chapters

The wizard uses your document’s headings to divide it into books, parts, chapters and scenes.
Under *Heading levels*, you can change what each heading level becomes. For example, if your
main headings are chapter titles, assign that level to *Chapter*.

If your files have chapter titles but no book title, *Add top level* adds a Book above them.
A file with no headings is imported as one item.

Books and parts cannot hold main text directly. When they have text to keep, *Its text* offers
*Keep as paratext* (the default) or *Drop the text*. Keep it unless you want to discard that
passage. [Paratext](:help-writing-model) includes writing such as a preface or dedication.

## What transfers

Word and LibreOffice files bring common text formatting, links, alignment, page breaks and
tables. Comments keep their authors and replies. Footnotes are imported too; endnotes become
footnotes, and the notes are renumbered.

The imported text may look different from the original document. Paragraph indentation and
justification follow your export style. Some formatting may be simplified.

*Text boxes, equations and embedded objects are left out.* Check the messages on *Review*
for anything that could not be imported, and copy any missing writing from the original file.
Symbols from picture fonts such as Wingdings may appear as empty boxes.

## Check messages before finishing

Messages below the preview explain anything changed or left out. In particular:

- If a comment cannot be attached to its original words, it may move to a nearby paragraph.
  Check its position after importing. Comments with no text to attach to are left out.
- A file that cannot be read is skipped; the other files can still be imported.
- Complex formatting, tables or deeply nested lists may be simplified.

For more about working with imported comments, see [comments](:help-comments).
